    <!-- About & Field Guide Screen -->
    <string name="about_title">About &amp; Field Guide</string>
    <string name="about_disclaimer">Independent application, not affiliated with the Modbus Organization.</string>
    <string name="about_version">Version %1$s · Pure Kotlin Modbus TCP/RTU Master</string>
    <string name="guide_rs485_title">1. RS485 Wiring (A/B, 120 Ω Termination &amp; Ground)</string>
    <string name="guide_rs485_body">• Polarity A/B (D+/D−): Formally in TIA/EIA-485, line B is non-inverting (+) and A is inverting (−), but many PLC, VFD, and meter manufacturers label A as D+ and B as D−. If you get timeouts, swap wires A and B first (this will not damage RS485 transceivers).\n• 120 Ω Termination: Place a 120 Ω resistor across A and B at the two physical ends of a long bus (or at high baud rates) to prevent signal reflections. For a short 1 m bench cable, termination is usually not required.\n• Common Reference: Connect signal ground (GND/COM) or shield at one point to keep common-mode voltage within −7 V to +12 V.</string>
    <string name="guide_address_title">2. 0-Based Protocol Address vs Modicon 40001</string>
    <string name="guide_address_body">• Inside a Modbus frame on the wire, addresses always start at 0 (0x0000).\n• Traditional Modicon documentation uses 1-based human numbering where the leading digit indicates the table: 0xxxx = Coils (FC01/05/15), 1xxxx = Discrete Inputs (FC02), 3xxxx = Input Registers (FC04), and 4xxxx = Holding Registers (FC03/06/16).\n• Therefore, Holding Register 40001 (5-digit) or 400001 (6-digit) is transmitted on the wire as Function Code 03 with protocol address 0 (0x0000), and 40108 becomes protocol address 107 (0x006B).</string>
    <string name="guide_endian_title">3. Float32 Word &amp; Byte Order (ABCD / CDAB / BADC / DCBA)</string>
    <string name="guide_endian_body">• Modbus registers are 16-bit big-endian words, so a 32-bit IEEE-754 float spans 2 consecutive registers.\n• ABCD (Big-Endian) sends the high word first and high byte first; CDAB (Word-Swapped) sends the low 16-bit word first and is very common in power meters and PLCs. This app displays all 4 orders simultaneously so you immediately spot the realistic engineering number.</string>
